%N A398216 Decimal expansion of the small face angle, in radians, of a canonical (dual-uniform) octagonal trapezohedron.
%C A398216 The octagonal trapezohedron is the dual polyhedron of the octagonal antiprism.
%C A398216 Its faces are kites with one acute angle (this constant) and three obtuse angles (A398217).
%H A398216 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A398216/b398216.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A398216 David I. McCooey, <a href="https://dmccooey.com/polyhedra/OctagonalTrapezohedron.html">Octagonal Trapezohedron</a>.
%H A398216 Polytope Wiki, <a href="https://polytope.miraheze.org/wiki/Octagonal_antitegum">Octagonal antitegum</a>.
%H A398216 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Trapezohedron.html">Trapezohedron</a>.
%H A398216 <a href="/index/Tra#transcendental">Index entries for transcendental numbers</a>.
%F A398216 Equals arccos(2 + 3/sqrt(2) - 3*cos(Pi/8) - sin(Pi/8)) = arccos(A176218 - 3*A144981 - A182168).
%F A398216 Equals arccos(c), where c = 0.966998... is the second largest root of 4*x^4 - 32*x^3 + 20*x^2 + 8*x - 1.
%e A398216 0.25762305595938721945332072673973864180963249235693...
%t A398216 First[RealDigits[ArcCos[2 + 3/Sqrt[2] - 3*Cos[Pi/8] - Sin[Pi/8]], 10, 100]]
%o A398216 (PARI) acos(2+3/sqrt(2)-3*cos(Pi/8)-sin(Pi/8)) \\ _Charles R Greathouse IV_, Aug 03 2026
